{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794014584,"nonce":"pqqBBnJulaIOmpTwLnyOCWf3DOo=","title":"Direct AIR-to-wasm backend, tiered wasm, and an embeddable runtime for headless renders","message":"Goal: wasm builds that do not wait on LLVM, and a runtime RayDE can embed to run an app and read its rendered frames back directly (no browser screenshot). Reference design: wren_lift (src/codegen/wasm.rs 3,770 lines via wasm_encoder; wasm/src/tier_up.rs 1,936; src/runtime/tier_wasm.rs 699). Related: 3b7fa54 (shard parse), 8b12bc8 (artifact reuse).\n\nWhat the tree says today\n- RayDE demo, 6.98 s warm build: LLVM backend 4.9 s, IR construction 0.9 s, AIR already optimized (docs/wasm/rayde-demo-build-times.md). The backend is the cost to remove.\n- wren_lift's wasm tier is a JIT: the VM runs as wasm in the page on its interpreter, hot methods are lowered MIR -\u003e wasm bytes, instantiated by the JS host as one small module each, called through a shared funcref table. No LLVM. Locals = SSA values; loops from back edges; functions the emitter or its gate refuses stay interpreted; no deopt; GC roots pushed explicitly around calls that can collect.\n- There is no interpreter in the page here: ash_browser only hosts AOT modules, and ash_interp has no wasm gating. The wren_lift model has no tier 0 in Ash.\n- The fiber transform (docs/internals/wasm-fibers.md) is per instance, seeds from AIR's own safepoints (hlp_fiber_poll at loop back edges, the yield import, blocking natives) and from an exact indirect-call target set, with the table fixed at minimum == maximum. A function added at runtime is outside all of that.\n- ash_wasm_link takes any number of objects, so a build can mix objects from two backends.\n- AIR v2 has about 60 instruction kinds (crates/air/src/v2/ir.rs). The LLVM lowering it replaces is about 14,000 lines (crates/ash/src/llvm/function), most of it natives, objects and calls.\n\nWhat LLVM does for wasm that a direct emitter must take over: structured control flow (including irreducible CFGs and multi-latch loops), local allocation, the C ABI with ash_runtime.o (a Rust-built object), wasm exception handling for traps (function/wasm_traps.rs), the relocatable-object format ash_wasm_link reads (including GOT.func for address-taken functions), thread TLS/atomics, feature selection (simd128, EH), line tables, and HashLink's own semantics where LLVM treats overflow, out-of-range fptosi and divide-by-zero as undefined and wasm traps. Not an LLVM gap: the fiber transform, which rewrites the final module.\n\nPlan\nP0, measure first (no emitter). Over the RayDE bin/*.hl corpus: AIR instruction kinds per function, count of functions with irreducible or multi-latch CFGs, functions that use traps, fraction of backend time spent in functions a simple subset covers. Output: numbers in this issue and a go/no-go. No speedup target is set before this.\nP1, direct object emitter in mixed mode, for AOT. AIR -\u003e relocatable wasm object with the same relocation and linking conventions as LLVM's, so ash_wasm_link, the runtime object and the fiber transform are unchanged. A function the emitter refuses goes to an LLVM shard; both objects are linked. Switch: --wasm-backend direct|llvm|mixed, default llvm until P1 is proven. Follow the existing poll/rooting contract exactly (docs/internals/wasm-fibers.md, wasm-threads.md); run GC stress. v1 refuses functions with traps; EH is a follow-up. Acceptance: modules validate (wasmparser); wasm conformance 1069/1069 under mixed; program output identical to the LLVM build on the corpus; build time and runtime speed both measured against llvm and against codegen level 0.\nP2, tiering. Decide after P1, with two options. A: in-page JIT as wren_lift does; needs the AIR pipeline and emitter shipped to the page, a tier 0 that starts instantly, table headroom, and the fiber state shared with emitted modules (the transform's globals are per instance today). B: service tiering: RayDE's host keeps the compiler, a fast direct build runs first, hot functions are rebuilt by LLVM in the background and swapped in through the existing side-module reload path. B needs far less in the page and is the recommended starting point. Either way a function added after link cannot suspend unless it is instrumented.\nP3, embedding API for RayDE. A library with: load module or bytecode, run, tick one frame, read the framebuffer as RGBA, inject input, capture console, swap code. Two hosts: native wasmtime plus native WebGPU (headless, CI-friendly) and the browser host (OffscreenCanvas, which only presents at the end of a task, so a frame must end with a yield). Acceptance: a Heaps/Ashui sample renders headless and matches a golden image from the browser build within a stated tolerance. Open: native WebGPU host for xgpu does not exist (wasmtime renders nothing today; see the sdl.hdll scoping notes).\n\nRisks: irreducible CFGs from HashLink bytecode; stale or mismatched ABI against ash_runtime.o; mixed-object symbol visibility and inlining lost across the backend boundary (the shard design already handles this for LLVM-to-LLVM); a second backend doubling the test surface.\n\nQuestions for the owner: is RayDE's embedding host native or browser; is an in-page interpreter wanted at all, or is option B enough; whether direct builds are for dev only.","files":null}]}